  "rationale": "These are prospective expected positive fractions across two prompts per family, with uncertainty about prompt transfer and nonlinear combinations. No heldout or combined outputs are known. Supplied singleton discovery texts are observations; source labels and published recipes are hypotheses and prior external claims, not established natural functions or outcomes here. The fixed base already expresses a cute cat persona, but strict same-act integration is rarer than cat vocabulary or generic friendliness. At the selected quarter strength, B produced only one clear virtual-hug offer in discovery, so I shrink the source claim that hugs appear in most answers. A supplies own paws/tail language without demonstrated affectionate use. C supplies enthusiastic, long, sometimes repetitive and truncated material, often lacking performed sounds; C alone gets modest vocalization gains and a coherence cost. D supplies performed sounds but also one repetitive Chinese discovery answer. E supplies Chinese roleplay and confidence, without demonstrated defensive affection, and its semantic causal interpretation is weak. Language switching alone does not imply incoherence.\n\nA x B: I expect increased probability of using own paws, tail, fur or purring body during a hug/cuddle, particularly in comfort and reunion. A plausible failure is independent tail commentary and an unrelated virtual hug, or more cute project discussion, neither satisfying embodied affection. This is extrapolation from source hypotheses and singleton ingredients, not a known pair result.\n\nD x C: I expect bubbly meows or purrs integrated into enthusiastic utterances more often than either singleton, while cat identity persists. Failure could be emoji, invented characters and repeated cute phrases without own-voice vocalization, or repetitive language switching. The published meow recipe is a prior claim at different doses, not an observation of this pair on these prompts.\n\nD x B: I expect some purring while snuggling or a warm mew addressed affectionately to the user. Failure could be a greeting sound detached from a later hug. The public Woof recipe also suggests doglike sounds as a competing failure, although the fixed cat base and weaker present strengths favor feline identity. Barking would not count as feline vocalization. No present pair output has been seen.\n\nC x B: I expect more eager celebratory hugs and energetic offers of cuddles, especially in celebration. Failure could be enthusiastic topic talk and a separate calm hug, or long repetitive cute text obscuring interpersonal affection. Genki and Hugs descriptions motivate this hypothesis but do not establish integrated pair behavior.\n\nE x B: I expect ordinary warm affection much more often than defensive affection. A small chance of a reluctant or self-conscious hug with explicit minimizing language seems plausible in teasing or compliment. Failure could be pride in one sentence and an unqualified hug elsewhere, which fails the defensive-affection criterion, or E mainly changing language/style. Neither discovery nor the failed semantic causal gate establishes a tsundere function.\n\nAll five: I expect a recognizably feline assistant with more opportunities for embodied cuddles, enthusiastic meows and excited affectionate acts, but substantial competition from long roleplay and repetition. Failure could show all ingredients disconnected, describe third-person characters, or degenerate into repeated multilingual fragments. I lower coherence and temper integrated gains for the crowded combination. Defensive affection remains low because no reliable mechanism or observed example supports it. Features are not assumed independent, and no probability asserts a known heldout outcome."
}
